/* Generated by tools/build.js from content/lessons/ai-900.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addLessons("ai-900", [{"t":"What AI is: models that learn patterns from data, and how that differs from rule-based software","body":["Artificial intelligence (AI) is software that imitates human abilities such as seeing, understanding language, making predictions and creating content. On AI-900 you are not expected to build AI from scratch. You are expected to recognize what kind of problem AI can solve, which Azure service fits, and what responsible use looks like. Almost everything in modern AI rests on one idea: a model that has learned patterns from data.","Traditional software is rule-based. A developer writes explicit logic, such as 'if the order total is over 100, apply free shipping'. That works when the rules are known and stable. It breaks down for tasks where nobody can write the rules, such as telling a cat from a dog in a photo or deciding whether a review sounds angry. There are too many variations to list.","Machine learning (ML) turns this around. Instead of writing the rules, you give an algorithm many examples of inputs and, usually, the correct outputs. Training adjusts the model's internal values until its outputs match the examples as closely as possible. The result is a model: a function that takes new input and returns a prediction. Using the trained model on new data is called inferencing (or inference).","Because a model learned from examples, its output is a probability-based best guess, not a guaranteed truth. A vision model might say 'dog, 94% confidence'. A language model might write a fluent paragraph that contains a mistake. This is why AI solutions need testing, human oversight and the responsible AI principles you will study later in this domain. It is also why the quality and representativeness of training data matter so much: a model can only learn patterns that are present in its data, including unwanted ones such as bias.","Most AI you will meet on the exam is delivered as a service. Azure AI services (Vision, Language, Speech, Translator, Document Intelligence, Content Safety and others) expose pretrained models through an API, so you send an image or text and get results back. Azure Machine Learning is for training your own models on your own data. Microsoft Foundry brings together generative AI models, agents and AI services in one portal. Knowing which of these to pick for a scenario is a large part of AI-900."],"terms":[["Model","A function learned from data that takes new input and returns a prediction, label, score or generated content."],["Training","The process of adjusting a model's internal values using example data until its outputs match the examples well."],["Inference","Using a trained model to make predictions on new data; also called inferencing or scoring."],["Rule-based software","Software whose behavior comes from explicit logic written by a developer rather than from patterns learned from data."]],"example":"A spam filter written with rules might block any email containing the word 'prize'. Spammers quickly change the wording. A machine learning spam filter is trained on thousands of emails already marked spam or not spam, learns hundreds of subtle signals, and keeps improving when it is retrained on newly reported messages.","tip":"If a scenario says the outcome is hard to express as rules and there is plenty of historical example data, the answer involves machine learning. Remember that model outputs are probabilistic, so answers claiming AI is always correct are wrong.","check":[["What is the difference between training and inference?","Training builds the model from example data; inference uses the trained model to make predictions on new data."],["Why can two similar inputs to an AI model give a wrong answer for one of them?","A model gives a probability-based prediction learned from its training data, not a guaranteed result, so unusual or poorly represented inputs can be misclassified."]]},{"t":"Common AI workloads: prediction and forecasting, anomaly detection, computer vision, NLP, document processing and generative AI","body":["A workload is a category of problem that AI solves. The first skill AI-900 measures is recognizing the workload behind a business scenario, because once you know the workload you can choose the technique and the Azure service. Exam questions usually describe a need in plain business language and ask which workload it is.","Prediction and forecasting use historical data to estimate an unknown or future value: next month's sales, the price a house will sell for, or whether a customer will cancel a subscription. These are classic machine learning tasks (regression for numbers, classification for categories). Anomaly detection learns what normal looks like and flags unusual events, such as a fraudulent card transaction, a sudden spike in server errors or a sensor reading that suggests a machine is about to fail.","Computer vision interprets images and video: classifying photos, detecting and locating objects, reading text in images (optical character recognition, OCR) and analyzing faces. Natural language processing (NLP) works with written language: detecting the language, judging sentiment, extracting key phrases and entities, summarizing and translating. Speech is closely related: converting speech to text and text to speech. Conversational AI combines language and speech to build bots and assistants that hold a dialog.","Document processing (often called document intelligence) extracts structured data from forms and documents such as invoices, receipts and ID cards: not just the text, but which value is the total and which is the due date. Knowledge mining goes further, extracting information from large volumes of unstructured content and building a searchable index, so people can find insights across thousands of files.","Generative AI creates new content in response to a prompt: text, code, images and more. It powers copilots and chat assistants that draft, summarize and answer questions, and agents that can also take actions. Unlike the other workloads, which analyze existing data, generative AI produces something that did not exist before, which brings its own risks such as fabricated answers.","Real solutions often combine workloads. A claims app might use OCR to read a form, entity recognition to find the policy number, anomaly detection to flag suspicious claims and a generative model to draft a letter. On the exam, focus on the main need described in the question."],"terms":[["Workload","A category of problem that AI addresses, such as computer vision, NLP or anomaly detection."],["Anomaly detection","Identifying data points or events that differ significantly from the normal pattern."],["Knowledge mining","Extracting information from large amounts of unstructured content and making it searchable."],["Generative AI","AI that creates new content, such as text, code or images, from a prompt."]],"example":"An airline uses forecasting to predict passenger numbers per flight, anomaly detection to spot unusual engine sensor data, computer vision to check baggage images, NLP to analyze complaint emails, and a generative AI assistant to help agents draft rebooking messages.","tip":"Look for the verb in the scenario: predict or estimate points to ML prediction, flag unusual points to anomaly detection, see or read an image points to computer vision, understand text points to NLP, extract fields from forms points to document processing, and create or draft points to generative AI.","check":[["A factory wants alerts when vibration readings on a motor look unusual. Which workload is this?","Anomaly detection, because it flags readings that differ from the normal pattern."],["What distinguishes generative AI from the other workloads?","It creates new content from a prompt, whereas the others analyze or extract information from existing data."]]},{"t":"Computer vision workloads: image classification, object detection, optical character recognition and facial analysis","body":["Computer vision is the area of AI that lets software interpret images and video. To a computer an image is just a grid of numbers (pixel values), so vision models learn which patterns of pixels correspond to meaningful things such as a cat, a stop sign or the letter A. Domain 3 covers the Azure services in detail; here you need to recognize the main vision workloads in a scenario."],"locked":true},{"t":"Natural language processing and speech workloads: text analysis, translation, speech recognition and conversational AI","body":["Natural language processing (NLP) is the area of AI that works with human language. It lets software make sense of emails, reviews, chat messages and documents, and respond in language people understand. Speech workloads extend this to spoken language. Domain 4 covers the Azure services; this lesson maps the workloads you will see in scenarios."],"locked":true},{"t":"Document processing and knowledge mining workloads: extracting fields from forms and making content searchable","body":["Organizations run on documents: invoices, receipts, purchase orders, tax forms, contracts, ID cards and medical records. Much of that information arrives as scans, photos or PDFs, and people spend hours typing it into systems. Document processing uses AI to read these documents and pull out the data automatically. Knowledge mining deals with the bigger problem of finding information across huge collections of content."],"locked":true},{"t":"Generative AI workloads: creating text, code and images from prompts, copilots and agents","body":["Generative AI is AI that creates new content. You give it a prompt, a natural language instruction or question, and it returns text, code, an image or other output that did not exist before. It is the fastest-growing area of AI and carries the largest weight on AI-900 (domain 5), so this lesson introduces the vocabulary."],"locked":true},{"t":"Responsible AI principles: fairness, and reliability and safety","body":["AI systems make or influence decisions that affect people: who gets a loan, which job applicants are shortlisted, how a car brakes. Microsoft groups its approach to responsible AI into six principles: fairness; reliability and safety; privacy and security; inclusiveness; transparency; and accountability. AI-900 tests whether you can match a scenario to the right principle, so this and the next two lessons take them two at a time."],"locked":true},{"t":"Responsible AI principles: privacy and security, and inclusiveness","body":["AI systems depend on data, often large amounts of personal data, and they increasingly shape how people access services. The principles of privacy and security and of inclusiveness address two sides of this: protecting the people whose data is used, and making sure the system works for everyone who needs it."],"locked":true},{"t":"Responsible AI principles: transparency and accountability","body":["The last two principles are about understanding and responsibility. Even a fair, safe and secure AI system can lose people's trust if nobody can explain what it does or who is answerable when it goes wrong. Transparency and accountability address those gaps."],"locked":true},{"t":"Responsible AI in practice: identifying harms, human oversight, Limited Access features and transparency notes","body":["Principles only help if they change how systems are built and run. This lesson covers the practical tools and habits that put responsible AI into action, including some specific Microsoft mechanisms that appear on AI-900."],"locked":true},{"t":"Features and labels, training and validation data, and how a model is trained and then used for inference","body":["Machine learning starts with data, usually arranged as a table. Each row is an observation (one house, one patient, one transaction) and each column is a value describing it. Understanding what the columns are for is the foundation of every other ML topic on AI-900."],"locked":true},{"t":"Regression: predicting a numeric value, with evaluation metrics MAE, RMSE and R²","body":["Regression is supervised machine learning that predicts a numeric value. How much will this house sell for? How many ice creams will we sell tomorrow? How many minutes will this delivery take? If the label is a number on a continuous scale, the task is regression."],"locked":true},{"t":"Binary and multiclass classification, with accuracy, precision, recall, F1 and the confusion matrix","body":["Classification is supervised machine learning that predicts which category (class) an item belongs to. Binary classification has two possible classes: spam or not spam, fraud or legitimate, will churn or will stay. Multiclass classification has three or more: which of five teams should get this ticket, which species is this flower, which of ten digits is handwritten here."],"locked":true},{"t":"Clustering: grouping unlabeled data, and supervised vs unsupervised learning","body":["Clustering is unsupervised machine learning that groups similar items together. Unlike regression and classification, there is no label column and no right answer in the training data. The algorithm looks only at the features and decides which items are alike."],"locked":true},{"t":"Deep learning: neural networks, weights and layers, and why they suit images, speech and language","body":["Deep learning is a branch of machine learning that uses artificial neural networks with many layers. It is behind nearly all modern computer vision, speech recognition and language models, including the large language models used in generative AI."],"locked":true},{"t":"The transformer architecture: tokens, embeddings and attention","body":["The transformer is the neural network architecture behind today's language models, including the GPT family used in Azure OpenAI. AI-900 expects you to know its main ideas at a conceptual level: tokens, embeddings and attention."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure Machine Learning: workspace, studio, data assets, compute instances and compute clusters","body":["Azure Machine Learning is Microsoft's cloud platform for data scientists and ML engineers to train, deploy and manage their own machine learning models. Where Azure AI services give you pretrained models through an API, Azure Machine Learning is for when you need a model trained on your own data for your own prediction task."],"locked":true},{"t":"Automated machine learning (AutoML) and the Azure Machine Learning designer","body":["Building a good model usually means trying many algorithms, data preparation steps and settings, then comparing the results. Azure Machine Learning offers two low-code ways to do this without writing training code yourself: automated machine learning and the designer. AI-900 asks you to recognize what each one is for."],"locked":true},{"t":"Deploying models to endpoints for real-time or batch inference, and responsible AI tools in Azure Machine Learning","body":["A model only creates value when applications can use it. In Azure Machine Learning, you deploy a trained model to an endpoint, a web address that accepts input data and returns predictions. AI-900 expects you to know the two main endpoint types and the responsible AI tools that help you check a model before and after deployment."],"locked":true},{"t":"Image classification vs object detection vs semantic segmentation","body":["Many computer vision questions on AI-900 come down to one decision: how much detail about the image does the scenario need? Is one answer for the whole image enough, does it need the location of each object, or does it need the exact shape of each object? Those three levels are image classification, object detection and semantic segmentation."],"locked":true},{"t":"How computer vision models work: pixels, filters, convolutional neural networks and multimodal models","body":["To a computer, a digital image is an array of numbers. A grayscale image is a grid of pixel values, typically from 0 (black) to 255 (white). A color image has three such grids, one each for red, green and blue channels. Every computer vision technique starts from these numbers."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Vision image analysis: captions, dense captions, tags, object detection, people detection and smart crops","body":["Azure AI Vision is the Azure AI service for analyzing images and video. Its image analysis feature gives you pretrained vision capabilities through a single API call: you send an image (as a URL or the image bytes) and say which visual features you want back. No training is needed. AI-900 expects you to match each feature to a scenario."],"locked":true},{"t":"Optical character recognition (OCR) with the Azure AI Vision Read feature","body":["Optical character recognition (OCR) is the computer vision task of detecting and extracting text from images. It turns pixels that look like letters into machine-readable text that can be searched, stored, translated or analyzed. In Azure, the main OCR capability is the Read feature, available through Azure AI Vision and also used inside Azure AI Document Intelligence."],"locked":true},{"t":"Face detection and analysis with Azure AI Face, and the Limited Access policy for identification and verification","body":["Azure AI Face is the Azure AI service for detecting and analyzing human faces in images. It is a good example of how capability and responsibility go together in AI-900: you need to know what the service does and which parts are restricted."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Document Intelligence: prebuilt models (invoices, receipts, IDs), the layout model and custom models","body":["Azure AI Document Intelligence (formerly Form Recognizer) is the Azure AI service for extracting text, structure and data from documents. It builds on OCR but returns much more: the layout of the page and, with the right model, named fields with values, ready to store in a database or pass to a business system."],"locked":true},{"t":"Creating and using Azure AI services resources: multi-service vs single-service resources, endpoints, keys and the free F0 tier","body":["Before your app can call any Azure AI service, you need a resource for it in your Azure subscription. The resource gives you an endpoint to call, credentials to authenticate and a place for billing. AI-900 tests the choices you make when creating one."],"locked":true},{"t":"Choosing the right Azure vision service for a scenario","body":["Many AI-900 vision questions describe a business need and ask which service or feature to use. The fastest way to answer is to identify the input and the output the scenario wants, then match them to the service. This lesson brings the domain together as a decision guide."],"locked":true},{"t":"Language detection, sentiment analysis and opinion mining","body":["Azure AI Language is the Azure AI service for understanding and analyzing text. It offers a set of prebuilt features that you call through one resource and one API, plus features you can customize. This lesson covers three of the most tested prebuilt features: language detection, sentiment analysis and opinion mining."],"locked":true},{"t":"Key phrase extraction, named entity recognition, entity linking and PII detection","body":["The next group of Azure AI Language features pulls specific information out of text. They answer questions like 'what is this about?', 'who and what is mentioned?' and 'is there personal data here?'. All are prebuilt, so you call them without training anything."],"locked":true},{"t":"Tokenization, embeddings and semantic similarity: how text becomes numbers","body":["Machine learning models work with numbers, not words. Every NLP system, from a sentiment classifier to a large language model, must first turn text into numeric form. AI-900 expects you to understand the main steps conceptually: tokenization, frequency-based representations and embeddings."],"locked":true},{"t":"Summarization and custom text classification in Azure AI Language","body":["Beyond the prebuilt analysis features, Azure AI Language can condense long text into summaries and can learn your own categories for classifying text. These two capabilities appear in scenarios about handling large volumes of documents and messages."],"locked":true},{"t":"Speech recognition (speech to text) and speech synthesis (text to speech) with Azure AI Speech","body":["Azure AI Speech is the Azure AI service for working with spoken language. Its two core capabilities are speech recognition, which turns audio into text, and speech synthesis, which turns text into audio. Many voice solutions use both."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure AI Translator for text and documents, and speech translation","body":["Translation is one of the oldest AI workloads, and modern neural machine translation has made it good enough for everyday business use. In Azure, text and document translation is provided by Azure AI Translator, while translation of spoken audio is provided by Azure AI Speech."],"locked":true},{"t":"Conversational language understanding: utterances, intents and entities","body":["When people talk to an app in their own words, the app must work out what they want and pull out the details it needs. Conversational language understanding (CLU), a feature of Azure AI Language, does this. It is the successor to the older Language Understanding (LUIS) service, which was retired."],"locked":true},{"t":"Question answering: building a knowledge base from FAQs for a bot","body":["Many organizations already have the answers customers need, spread across FAQ pages, product manuals and help articles. Question answering, a feature of Azure AI Language, turns that content into a knowledge base that a bot or app can query in natural language. It replaced the older QnA Maker service."],"locked":true},{"t":"Choosing the right Azure language or speech service for a scenario","body":["Domain 4 questions often describe a need and ask which Azure service or feature to use. As with vision, the reliable method is to identify the input (text or audio) and the output the scenario wants, then map them. This lesson pulls the domain together as a decision guide."],"locked":true},{"t":"How large language models generate text: tokens, next-token prediction and pretraining","body":["Large language models (LLMs) are the engines behind most generative AI. They are transformer-based neural networks with billions of parameters, trained on enormous amounts of text. Understanding how they produce text explains both what they are good at and why they sometimes get things wrong."],"locked":true},{"t":"Common generative AI scenarios: copilots, chat assistants, content drafting, summarization, code and image generation","body":["Generative AI is used across nearly every industry, but most applications fall into a handful of scenario types. AI-900 asks you to recognize them and to see where generative AI fits better or worse than traditional AI services."],"locked":true},{"t":"Prompt engineering: system messages, user prompts, few-shot examples and clear instructions","body":["A prompt is everything you send a generative model to get a response. Prompt engineering is the practice of designing prompts that produce accurate, relevant and consistently formatted output. It is the cheapest and fastest way to improve a generative AI app, before considering grounding or fine-tuning."],"locked":true},{"t":"Grounding and retrieval augmented generation (RAG) with your own data","body":["A large language model knows only what was in its training data. It has never seen your company's policies, product catalogue or last week's announcements, and it may confidently invent an answer when asked. Grounding solves this by giving the model relevant, trusted information in the prompt and instructing it to base its answer on that information."],"locked":true},{"t":"Model settings: temperature, top_p and maximum response length","body":["Besides the prompt, you can change how a generative model chooses its words with a few request settings (often called parameters). They are visible in the Microsoft Foundry chat playground and can be set in every API call. AI-900 expects you to know what the common ones do and when to change them."],"locked":true},{"t":"Microsoft Foundry (formerly Azure AI Foundry): hubs and projects, the model catalog and the playgrounds","body":["Microsoft Foundry is Microsoft's platform and portal for building generative AI apps and agents on Azure. It was previously called Azure AI Studio and then Azure AI Foundry, and you may still see those names in older training material and exam questions. Product names in this area change often; the concepts below are what matter."],"locked":true},{"t":"Azure OpenAI models in Foundry: GPT chat models, embeddings models and image generation models","body":["Azure OpenAI gives organizations access to OpenAI's models, hosted in Azure. It is available through Microsoft Foundry, where Azure OpenAI models appear in the model catalog alongside models from other providers. What Azure adds is enterprise hosting: Azure identity and access control, private networking options, regional deployment choices, built-in content filtering and Microsoft's data protection commitments."],"locked":true},{"t":"AI agents: models with instructions, tools and knowledge, and the Foundry Agent Service","body":["An AI agent is a generative AI application that can reason about a goal and act to achieve it, not just reply with text. Agents are one of the fastest-growing parts of generative AI and a named topic in AI-900's generative AI domain."],"locked":true},{"t":"Responsible generative AI: identify, measure, mitigate and operate; content filters and Azure AI Content Safety","body":["Generative AI brings new kinds of risk: output that is harmful, biased, false or leaks information, and users who try to misuse the system. Microsoft's guidance for responsible generative AI organizes the work into four stages: identify, measure, mitigate and operate. AI-900 tests the stages and the Azure tools that support them."],"locked":true},{"t":"Evaluating generative AI apps: groundedness, relevance, fluency and safety evaluations, and red teaming","body":["Traditional ML models are evaluated with metrics like accuracy or RMSE against known labels. Generative AI output is open-ended text, so there is rarely one right answer to compare with. Evaluation therefore uses a mix of quality metrics, safety metrics, human review and adversarial testing, and it continues after release."],"locked":true}], {"reviewed":"2026-09-25"});
